  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge extends from Texas to the Upper Midwest with an \nupper trough just behind the upper ridge. Winds across south central \nand central Nebraska and north central Kansas are gusting out of the \nsouth in response to a strengthening surface trough across western \nNebraska and eastern Colorado. Wind gusts up to 35 to 45 MPH are \nexpected this afternoon across portions of the area. Temperatures \nthis afternoon are expected to warm up into the mid 70s to lower \n80s. Winds will weaken some this evening (around 15 to 20 MPH \nsustained) and moreso late tonight (around 5 to 10 MPH) but will \nremain out of the south. Low temperatures tonight will be in the low \n50s to low 60s. Light rain showers may develop across portions of \nthe area this afternoon in response to a near shortwave trough, but \nthe main development of rain and possibly isolated thunderstorms is \nnot expected until later this evening into tonight. \n\nA cold front will begin to push into the area on Saturday as the \nupper trough begins to move over the region. Rain and storm chances \nwill increase on Saturday (up to around 70% in some areas) near and \nbehind the front. High temperatures on Saturday are somewhat \nuncertain due to strength and timing of the front but generally \nexpect temperatures to range from the low 60s to upper 70s. Low \ntemperatures Saturday night are expected to range from the mid/upper \n30s to mid 50s. The southern portion of the previously mentioned \nupper trough will still be to the west and southwest of the area \n(just beginning to move over Nebraska and Kansas) on Sunday which \nwill keep upper lift over the area. This will result in more rain \n(and possibly isolated storm) chances on Sunday and Sunday night \n(chances ranging from around 40% to near 100%). Temperature \nuncertainty will continue into Sunday but generally expect high \ntemperatures to range from the mid 50s to mid 70s. The upper trough \nis expected to be over the region on Monday allowing for rain and \nstorm chances to continue (around 60% to 90%). High temperatures on \nMonday will mainly be in the 50s and 60s. Low temperatures Monday \nnight are expected to range from the low 30s to low 50s. \n\nHigh temperatures in the 50s and 60s will continue for Tuesday with \nmostly dry conditions. There may be a slight warm up for Wednesday \n(highs in the 60s and 70s) with temperatures cooling again for \nThursday (highs in the 50s and 60s). Low temperatures Tuesday night \nand Wednesday night will generally be in the mid 30s to low 50s. \nVarious low-end (15% to near 30%) precipitation chances will be \npresent Tuesday night through Thursday night.",
